#### A monopoly utility asked a federal judge to force two Arkansas newspapers to unpublish a story. The story: that ordinary ratepayers are footing two-thirds of the bill for the power grid Google needs for its new data center, under a contract the state kept secret for nine months. The judge said no. That should be the end of it. It isn't.

> **corporatocracy** *(noun)* — a system of government in which corporations, financial institutions, or other business entities hold effective political power, either by directly controlling the state or by exerting decisive influence over the officials and institutions that do.

***Editorial note:** the reporting below is laid out as straight as I can*, with every figure attributed to its source*, including the places where the utility and the newspaper it sued don't agree on the math. The passages that are clearly mine — what I think this means — are marked as such. I'm not neutral on whether a company should be allowed to sue a newspaper into silence over a story about your electric bill. Nobody should be.*

## What happened

On September 1, 2026, Entergy Arkansas — the monopoly electric utility for most of the state — filed an emergency motion in federal court asking a judge to order the *Arkansas Democrat-Gazette* to take down a story from its website and to bar both the *Democrat-Gazette* and the *Arkansas Times* from publishing anything further about Entergy's power contract with a Google subsidiary building a data center in West Memphis. Two days later, on September 2, U.S. District Judge Lee Rudofsky denied the request. He found that ordering two newspapers to unpublish, or refrain from publishing, true information about a matter of intense public interest — what people's electric bills are paying for — would be an unconstitutional prior restraint on the press. Entergy told the court the next day it would not pursue a preliminary injunction, though it said it planned to amend its complaint, meaning this fight isn't necessarily over.

Strip away the legal terminology, and here's what actually happened: a company tried to use a federal court to make a true story disappear because the story embarrassed it. That's the whole shape of this piece. Everything below is what's in the story Entergy didn't want you to read, and what it took to get it in front of you anyway.

## Who these companies actually are

**Entergy Arkansas** is not a small player asking for a favor. It's a subsidiary of Entergy Corporation, a Fortune 500 holding company headquartered in New Orleans that owns five regulated, vertically integrated electric utilities across Arkansas, Louisiana, Mississippi, and Texas, serving more than 3 million customers. Entergy Arkansas alone serves roughly 730,000 residential, commercial, and industrial customers across 63 counties. As a regulated monopoly, Entergy Arkansas doesn't compete for those customers — if you live in its territory, it's the only company you're legally allowed to buy electricity from, and the only mechanism you have to influence what you pay is a state commission that reviews its rate requests. That commission is discussed below, and it isn't much of a check.

**Google's** data center in this deal isn't even listed in the underlying contracts under Google's own name. The agreements were struck with Altitude Capital LLC, a Google subsidiary set up specifically to build out the project — a detail worth sitting with on its own, since it means the company whose product you use every day isn't the one named on the power contract driving up your neighbor's electric bill. Google first announced the $4 billion West Memphis data center campus — five buildings on roughly 1,100 to 1,200 acres off Bollinger Road, depending on which outlet's figure you use — in October 2025, calling it Arkansas's largest investment ever from a single company.

## The deal nobody outside a courtroom was supposed to see

To power that campus, Entergy is building Cypress Solar, a 600-megawatt solar field paired with a 350-megawatt battery storage system in Jefferson County, which connects to Entergy's White Bluff substation and is expected to begin service in February 2027, ramping to full capacity by October 2029. The whole facility is projected to cost $1.6 billion.

According to a Public Service Commission memo obtained through the records fight described below, Google is paying $526 million of that directly — roughly a third of the construction cost — plus another $190 million for transmission upgrades needed to connect the campus to the grid, for a combined $716 million in direct infrastructure payments from Google. That much isn't seriously disputed by either side.

What happens to the other two-thirds of the project's cost is the part Entergy fought hardest to keep out of the papers. Filings submitted to the Arkansas Public Service Commission show that gap gets closed through a rate rider charged to ordinary customers — an increase filings peg at between $5.77 and $8.35 a month by year five, commonly rounded in reporting to "nearly $6 a month," layered on top of a separate, broader rate increase Entergy has pending before the same commission, one that's already drawing formal objections from Walmart and other Arkansas business groups. Entergy counters that the arrangement, taken as a whole, delivers Arkansas ratepayers more than $1.1 billion in net benefits over the 20-year contract term by lowering the utility's overall system costs. That claim comes from Entergy's own regulatory filings, not from an independent audit, and this piece treats it accordingly — reported, not verified as fact by this outlet.

There's also a genuine, unresolved fight over the total price tag. Entergy's public rebuttal to the newspapers' reporting does its own math — $443 million in what it calls accelerated payments, plus $83 million a year in minimum demand payments over the full 20-year term — and arrives at roughly $2.1 billion in total Google payments over the life of the contract, a materially larger number than the $716 million infrastructure figure above. The *Democrat-Gazette* pushed back hard on Entergy's rebuttal in its own statement, saying the utility "operates on a fallacious and unchecked assumption that the figure in question was the result of a mathematical calculation. This is untrue." The paper said Entergy "never inquired with the Arkansas Democrat-Gazette about the source of the number" — that the $526 million figure came directly from the leaked PSC documents, not from the newspaper's own math, and that "independent of us, Google repeatedly has cited the same figure of $526 million to other news outlets as well as to the Arkansas Democrat-Gazette." The paper's conclusion: "Entergy, in fact, performs calculations in its statement that we have not seen substantiated by the records." Neither figure has been independently audited by this outlet. Both are presented here with their source clearly attached, because the honest answer to "how much is Google really paying" is that the two parties to the contract don't agree on the number in public, and a company that just tried to gag the newspaper checking its math has, at minimum, forfeited the benefit of the doubt on which number to trust by default.

Google, for its part, put a spokesperson on the record. "We are fully committed to covering 100% of the power and infrastructure costs for our West Memphis data center," Google's Jessica Ryan said in a statement. "While upfront payments total $526 million, our ongoing monthly rates over the 20-year agreement will completely fund our operational footprint." Google separately pledged $25 million toward a community energy fund to help low-income Crittenden County residents weatherize their homes — a real commitment, and also, next to a $716 million infrastructure deal and a multi-year rate rider on 730,000 customers, a small one.

## How a nurse's records request blew the lid off

The Public Service Commission approved Entergy's special rate contract with Google in December 2025, while ordering Entergy to refile part of its accounting — and kept the underlying contract terms confidential, at both companies' request, as protected trade secrets. The *Democrat-Gazette* had been trying to pry the numbers loose through ordinary channels for months, including a state-court lawsuit against the PSC itself seeking the unredacted contract; a judge dismissed that suit on July 28, 2026.

What actually got the documents into public view was a private citizen. Jessica Kivell, a nurse in central Arkansas, filed her own Freedom of Information Act request with the Public Service Commission. The commission's staff released the records to her — and this time, whether by oversight or a narrower reading of what counted as protected, the release included figures Entergy says should have stayed confidential. Kivell passed what she'd received to reporters. The *Democrat-Gazette* published its first story on the numbers on August 31; Entergy was in federal court trying to force it offline within 48 hours.

Austin Gelder, editor of the *Arkansas Times* — which was named in Entergy's lawsuit despite not having possessed the leaked documents itself — put the sequence plainly after the ruling: "While the Arkansas Public Service Commission might have handed over the information by mistake, Kivell made no mistake in recognizing this was important stuff that her fellow citizens deserved to know about."

Kivell herself was also named in Entergy's restraining-order request. According to the *Arkansas Times*' own account of the hearing, relayed by the Daily Caller, Kivell — a nurse and mother — showed up to the federal courthouse in her work scrubs on September 1 after arranging for her husband to pick up their daughter, and was "visibly scared" through the proceeding, weeping during a closed-door portion of the hearing and continuing to cry when she returned to the courtroom. A private citizen who filed a routine public-records request ended up in federal court, in tears, because a Fortune 500 utility didn't like what the documents she received turned out to say.

## What the judge actually said

Judge Rudofsky's reasoning tracked a specific, decades-old First Amendment principle: once true information of public concern has been lawfully obtained by a news outlet — even if a government agency released it by its own mistake — the press generally cannot be barred from publishing it. Legal commentary on the ruling cited the Supreme Court's 1989 decision in&nbsp;*Florida Star v. B.J.F.*&nbsp;as the controlling precedent. Seth Stern, chief of advocacy at the Freedom of the Press Foundation, called the outcome "a relief" and added a sharper note: "It would be even better if judges would sanction litigants and lawyers who waste the time and resources of both the judiciary and the press with these unconstitutional demands to gag journalists."

One detail is worth stating plainly because it runs counter to the easy version of this story: Judge Rudofsky was appointed to the federal bench by Donald Trump and has since been nominated by the same administration to a seat on the 8th U.S. Circuit Court of Appeals. He is not, by any obvious measure, a judge inclined to rule against corporate interests as a matter of ideology. He ruled against Entergy anyway because the law was not unclear. That's what a judge doing the job honestly looks like, and it's worth naming him for getting this one right, in a piece that's otherwise not inclined to hand out compliments to power.

*It also sets the bar for what comes next, since Entergy has signaled it isn't finished litigating. Any judge — on this case, on appeal, or on the next one just like it somewhere else — who rules the other way, who decides a company's discomfort at being caught outweighs the public's right to know what it's paying for, will not be applying an unsettled area of law. They'll be choosing the utility over the public, even though the legal standard already points the other way. That's not a close call to get right. A judge who gets it wrong isn't making a judgment call. They're picking a side.*

## This isn't just Arkansas

Entergy's instinct to keep the Google contract behind a wall of confidentiality isn't an isolated overreach — it's standard practice in the data center industry right now. NPR reported on August 27, 2026, that tech companies building AI data centers routinely bind local officials and economic development staff to non-disclosure agreements before talks even begin, a pattern documented across multiple states, including at least four Wisconsin municipalities that signed NDAs with Microsoft and Meta. A Newsweek analysis separately mapped states in which entire data center projects have been shielded from public view by such agreements. Google itself has fought to keep water-usage figures for another data center in Virginia secret, in a separate case. What makes the Arkansas case unusual isn't the secrecy — it's that Entergy escalated from confidentiality agreements to a federal lawsuit aimed directly at the press, once the secrecy failed.

## Where the national press was

Here's the part that should bother you almost as much as the lawsuit itself. A Fortune 500 utility tried to get a federal court to force two newspapers to unpublish a true story and to bar them from further reporting on a matter directly affecting their electric bills. That is, by any normal definition, a national press-freedom story — the kind of prior-restraint fight that usually generates wall-to-wall cable coverage and a dozen op-eds within a day. Instead, outside of Arkansas's own papers, the legal trade press, and a handful of national outlets that happened to be covering the data-center-and-utilities beat already — CNBC, Axios, a syndicated wire story or two — this barely registered. No sustained national outrage cycle. No prime-time segment. The same national press apparatus that will spend a week on a press-freedom dispute with an obvious partisan hook largely let this one pass with a shrug because the villain was a utility company and a trillion-dollar tech company rather than a more familiar political target. If the First Amendment only reliably makes the news when the defendant is politically convenient, that's not principled press advocacy. That's a beat being covered selectively, and it's worth naming as its own failure, sitting right next to Entergy's.

## The state government's role

The Arkansas Public Service Commission approved this contract in December 2025 and kept its terms confidential for 9 months, until a citizen's records request forced the issue. All three PSC commissioners are gubernatorial appointees — the commission currently includes Chairman Doyle Webb, appointed by Governor Sarah Huckabee Sanders in January 2023, as well as commissioners serving terms set by the governor's office. Entergy said in its own public statement that the contract was never confidential "to the public officials who regulate state utilities and protect the public interest, including the Arkansas Public Service Commission and its Staff and the Arkansas Attorney General" — which is, if anything, a worse look than secrecy alone: the state's own regulators saw the full numbers, signed off on keeping them from the ratepayers footing the bill, and left it to a nurse with a FOIA request to do the job of public accountability the commission is funded to do itself.

## Where is the governor?

As of this writing, Governor Sarah Huckabee Sanders has not publicly addressed Entergy's lawsuit against the newspapers and Jessica Kivell — no statement, no press office comment, nothing found in the reporting reviewed for this piece, despite the story generating national pickup (CNBC, Axios, the Daily Caller, multiple press-freedom organizations) over more than a week. That silence is notable given how present she's been on the underlying deal. Sanders has publicly celebrated Arkansas's data-center courtship, saying she hopes "all the other states keep saying we don't want data centers" so more of them land in Arkansas, and her office has credited her 2025 Generating Arkansas Jobs Act — which cut permitting timelines and created tax incentives — with helping secure the Google project in the first place. The Public Service Commission that approved the underlying Entergy-Google contract in secret is composed entirely of her own appointees, currently chaired by Doyle Webb. None of that means she personally directed Entergy's decision to sue a nurse and two newspapers. It does mean the regulatory and legislative groundwork that made this deal, and its secrecy, possible came out of her administration — and that a governor who was quick to take a public victory lap over landing the deal has so far said nothing about a Fortune 500 utility trying to gag the press over how that deal actually works.

## Three honest ways to read this

**The system worked** eventually because someone outside it refused to let it fail**.** The PSC kept this contract secret. Entergy tried to keep the leak from reaching the public. A federal judge stopped that specific effort. None of that happens without Jessica Kivell filing a records request as a private citizen and two newsrooms deciding it was worth the legal risk to publish. The system's safeguards held this time, but they held because people outside any official process forced them to.

**The dollar figures are genuinely disputed, and that's not a reason to look away from the story.** Entergy and the newspaper that broke it don't agree on whether Google's total 20-year payment is closer to $716 million or $2.1 billion. This piece isn't resolving that dispute for you because it can't be resolved honestly without documents that neither side has fully put in the public view. What isn't in dispute is that ordinary customers are paying a rate rider for part of a data center's power buildout, and that the company being asked to pay it tried to stop you from finding out the terms.

**The lawsuit against the press may matter more than the rate hike itself in the long term.** A $6-a-month increase is a real burden on many households, but it's a fight that plays out through the normal regulatory process, in public, with the PSC's Nov. 4–5 hearing as the next checkpoint. A company suing newspapers to block true reporting is a different kind of problem — it's an attempt to disable the mechanism that would let the public find out about the next rate hike, and the one after that, before it's a done deal.

## What you can actually do with this

- The PSC takes up Entergy's broader rate case on November 4–5, 2026, and can question the utility directly on the Google buildout, customer costs, and its push for higher base rates. Public comment to the commission is on the record and part of that proceeding.
- If your utility, anywhere, signs an economic-development deal with a data center developer, ask directly whether the agreement is subject to an NDA, and whether your state's public-records law has an exception for "trade secrets" broad enough to keep the terms from you. Most states do.
- Support outlets willing to publish when a well-funded party tells them not to. The *Arkansas Democrat-Gazette* and *Arkansas Times* both took on real legal risk and legal fees to keep this story up. That's the actual, unglamorous cost of the First Amendment working as intended.

## A closing word

*I don't think Entergy did anything here that most large, monopoly-protected utilities wouldn't do if they thought they could get away with it. That's exactly the problem. Google got a heavily subsidized, aggressively negotiated power buildout and let a subsidiary's name take the public heat for it. Entergy took the deal, kept it secret with the state's blessing, and when the secret got out anyway, tried to use a federal court to put it back in the box. The Arkansas Public Service Commission — three people appointed by one governor — signed off on keeping 730,000 ratepayers in the dark about their own bills, and only backed off because a nurse and two newsrooms made it too public to sustain. And the national press, which will cover a press-freedom fight for a week when the villain fits a familiar script, let this one go by almost silently, because the villain here was a utility monopoly and a trillion-dollar tech company instead. Every one of those failures is fixable by people who pay attention and refuse to let it go quiet. This time, it worked, by the narrowest possible margin — one judge, one ruling, one nurse's records request. That's not a system you can count on. That's a system you got lucky with once. Don't mistake the one win for the fight being over.*

